.TH PASSGEN 1 "@DATE@" "passgen @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
passgen \- generate secure random passwords locally
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B passgen
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B passgen \-i
.br
.B passgen check
.RB [ \-\-min\-bits
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.br
.B passgen completion
.BR bash | zsh | fish
.br
.B passgen man
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B passgen
makes random passwords with the operating system's cryptographic random
source.
It runs entirely offline: it never touches the network, except when asked to
with
.BR \-\-check\-update .
.PP
By default it prints one 16\-character password from lowercase, uppercase,
digits and the
.I safe
symbol set, with at least one character from each.
That is about 99 bits of entropy.
The password goes to standard output; a strength line goes to standard error,
so piping or capturing the output gets only the password.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-length " \fIN\fR"
Password length, 1\-4096 (default 16).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-count " \fIN\fR"
Number of passwords, 1\-10000 (default 1).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-complexity " \fILEVEL\fR"
Character set preset:
.B low
(a\-z 0\-9),
.B medium
(adds A\-Z) or
.B high
(adds symbols; the default).
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-symbol\-mode " \fIMODE\fR"
Which symbols to use (default
.BR safe ):
.RS
.TP 10
.B safe
.B \-_.+=@#%*?^
\(em accepted almost everywhere; no quoting trouble in shells, JSON, XML,
URLs, CSV, SQL or .env files.
.TP
.B standard
.B !@#$%^&*()\-_=+[]{};:,.<>?/~
\(em wider; still no quotes, backslash, backtick, pipe or space.
.TP
.B complex
all 32 printable ASCII symbols; some systems reject or mangle a few of them.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-symbol\-set " CHARS"
Use exactly these symbol characters (overrides
.BR \-\-symbol\-mode ).
.TP
.BR \-\-lower ", " \-\-no\-lower
Include or exclude a\-z, overriding the preset.
.TP
.BR \-\-upper ", " \-\-no\-upper
Include or exclude A\-Z.
.TP
.BR \-\-digits ", " \-\-no\-digits
Include or exclude 0\-9.
.TP
.BR \-\-symbols ", " \-\-no\-symbols
Include or exclude symbols.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ambiguous
Leave out look\-alike characters:
.B Il1O0o|`'"
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " CHARS"
Leave out these characters too.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-require\-each
Don't force one character from each enabled set.
.TP
.B \-\-copy
Copy the first password to the clipboard (pbcopy, clip, wl\-copy, xclip or
xsel).
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print passwords only, without the strength line.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Ask for the options, then loop: Enter makes new passwords,
.B c
or a number copies one,
.B s
changes the settings,
.B q
quits.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-symbols
Show the symbol sets and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-update
Ask GitHub whether a newer release exists and print where to get it.
This is the only time passgen uses the network.
It never downloads or installs anything.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help text.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check
Estimate the strength of an existing password, offline.
The password is read from standard input: type it at the prompt (it is not
shown on screen on macOS, Linux and BSD) or pipe it in.
It is never taken as an argument, since that would put it in your shell
history.
.RS
.PP
The estimate starts from brute force and charges far less for what cracking
tools try first: common passwords and words (also with l33t swaps), repeated
characters, sequences such as
.B abcd
or
.BR 4321 ,
keyboard runs such as
.BR qwerty ,
and repeats of an earlier part.
Making a pattern longer barely raises the estimate.
The report never shows any part of the password.
Input is limited to 4096 characters.
It is a best case: real tools use much bigger word lists.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-bits " N"
Exit with status 3 if the estimate is below
.I N
bits.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print only the estimated bits.
.RE
.TP
.BR completion " " bash | zsh | fish
Print a shell completion script.
.TP
.B man
Print this manual page.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid options, or an error such as no clipboard tool.
.TP
.B 2
Command line could not be parsed.
.TP
.B 3
.B check
estimate below
.BR \-\-min\-bits .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B passgen \-l 24 \-n 5
Five 24\-character passwords.
.TP
.B passgen \-c medium \-\-no\-ambiguous
Letters and digits only, without look\-alikes.
.TP
.B passgen \-s complex \-l 32 \-\-copy
A 32\-character password with every symbol, copied to the clipboard.
.TP
.B pbpaste | passgen check \-\-min\-bits 80
Check the password on the clipboard; exit 3 if it is weaker than 80 bits.
.TP
.B source <(passgen completion bash)
Turn on tab completion in the current bash shell.
.SH SEE ALSO
https://github.com/bankierubybank/passgen
